# Walkthrough - Fixing MCP Config Error

I have corrected the configuration in [mcp_config.json](file:///c:/Users/Deacjx/.gemini/antigravity/mcp_config.json) to resolve the schema validation error and ensure all servers are correctly registered.

## Changes Made

### Configuration Fixes
#### [mcp_config.json](file:///c:/Users/Deacjx/.gemini/antigravity/mcp_config.json)
- **Removed Invalid Property**: The `"path"` property was removed from the `dart-mcp-server` configuration as it is not part of the MCP schema.
- **Absolute Command Path**: Updated the `"command"` for `dart-mcp-server` to point to the correct executable found in the Flutter cache: [C:\flutter\bin\cache\dart-sdk\bin\dart.exe](file:///flutter/bin/cache/dart-sdk/bin/dart.exe). The previous path was incorrect for this system.
- **Structural Cleanup**: Moved `cloudrun` and `firebase-mcp-server` from the top-level of the JSON file into the `"mcpServers"` object, which is the correct parent for server definitions.

## Verification Results

### Manual Verification
- Checked the JSON structure to ensure it matches the expected MCP configuration format.
- Verified that `dart-mcp-server` now uses a valid command path.
- Confirmed that all servers are now logically grouped under `mcpServers`.

> [!TIP]
> You may need to restart your MCP client (or the application using these servers) for the changes to take effect.
